{
  "note": "Dorsal and Ventral Visual Pathways",
  "source": "3e4ef788f98f",
  "script": "f3c1b922ac83",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "7ac849e5624f",
  "duration": 214.7,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Dorsal and Ventral Visual Pathways",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Beyond the primary visual cortex, or V one, vision is sorted into two largely separate cortical pathways.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 7.2
        },
        {
          "text": "The magnocellular pathway continues as the dorsal pathway to the posterior parietal cortex, and it serves spatial vision.",
          "start": 7.52,
          "end": 15.08
        },
        {
          "text": "The parvocellular pathway, on the other hand, continues as the ventral pathway to the inferior temporal cortex, and it serves object recognition.",
          "start": 15.28,
          "end": 23.8
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll trace each stream from start to finish, and after that we'll see where the two of them lie on the brain.",
          "start": 24,
          "end": 29.68
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The magnocellular pathway",
      "start": 30.537,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Our first stream is the magnocellular pathway, which carries the output of the M ganglion cells onwards from V one.",
          "start": 30.537,
          "end": 37.857
        },
        {
          "text": "It relays from V one to visual area two, or V two, and on to visual area three, or V three.",
          "start": 37.897,
          "end": 45.057
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally, it reaches the middle temporal area, known as M T.",
          "start": 45.097,
          "end": 49.377
        },
        {
          "text": "From V one, it forms the dorsal pathway, sweeping up into the posterior parietal cortex.",
          "start": 49.417,
          "end": 55.377
        },
        {
          "text": "Along the way, each area adds its own analysis.",
          "start": 55.417,
          "end": 58.817
        },
        {
          "text": "V three processes the orientation and position of objects, whereas M T processes object movement.",
          "start": 58.857,
          "end": 65.537
        },
        {
          "text": "Taken as a whole, the stream provides spatial vision, meaning the detection of motion and change in the visual scene.",
          "start": 65.577,
          "end": 72.537
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The parvocellular pathway",
      "start": 73.3,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The second stream, the parvocellular pathway, carries the output of the P ganglion cells onwards from V one.",
          "start": 73.3,
          "end": 80.54
        },
        {
          "text": "This time the relay runs from V one to V two and on to visual area four, or V four.",
          "start": 80.58,
          "end": 87.1
        },
        {
          "text": "It forms the ventral pathway, which sweeps down into the inferior temporal cortex.",
          "start": 87.14,
          "end": 92.527
        },
        {
          "text": "For its part, V four analyses colour, form, shape and texture.",
          "start": 92.553,
          "end": 97.66
        },
        {
          "text": "Overall, the stream provides object recognition, that is, the perception of form and detail.",
          "start": 97.7,
          "end": 103.9
        },
        {
          "text": "V two itself, meanwhile, has no separate speciality.",
          "start": 103.94,
          "end": 107.46
        },
        {
          "text": "Like every extrastriate area, it contains its own map of visual space, and it is largely dependent on V one for its activation.",
          "start": 107.54,
          "end": 115.487
        },
        {
          "text": "What it does do is serve as the shared relay of both pathways, passing information on to the specialised areas that follow.",
          "start": 115.513,
          "end": 123.14
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The two pathways on the brain",
      "start": 123.899,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now let's place the two streams on the brain itself.",
          "start": 123.899,
          "end": 127.139
        },
        {
          "text": "V one sits at the occipital pole, and both pathways begin there before passing through V two, just anterior to it.",
          "start": 127.179,
          "end": 134.512
        },
        {
          "text": "The dorsal pathway runs from V one through V two to M T, at the junction of the occipital and temporal lobes, and then up into the parietal lobe.",
          "start": 134.566,
          "end": 143.859
        },
        {
          "text": "This is the route for spatial vision.",
          "start": 143.899,
          "end": 146.259
        },
        {
          "text": "The ventral pathway runs from V one through V two to V four, and then curves down and forwards into the temporal lobe.",
          "start": 146.299,
          "end": 154.019
        },
        {
          "text": "This one is the route for object recognition.",
          "start": 154.059,
          "end": 156.832
        },
        {
          "text": "The anatomical split therefore mirrors the functional one.",
          "start": 156.886,
          "end": 160.432
        },
        {
          "text": "Put simply, dorsal and parietal is for where things are and how they move, while ventral and temporal is for what things are.",
          "start": 160.486,
          "end": 168.299
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 169.064,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Bringing everything together, the magnocellular pathway relays from V one through V two and V three to M T.",
          "start": 169.064,
          "end": 176.544
        },
        {
          "text": "It forms the dorsal pathway to the posterior parietal cortex.",
          "start": 176.584,
          "end": 180.637
        },
        {
          "text": "In this stream, V three encodes orientation and position, and M T encodes object movement, so the stream gives spatial vision, motion and change.",
          "start": 180.691,
          "end": 190.877
        },
        {
          "text": "The parvocellular pathway relays from V one through V two to V four, and it forms the ventral pathway to the inferior temporal cortex.",
          "start": 190.931,
          "end": 199.864
        },
        {
          "text": "Within it, V four encodes colour, form, shape and texture, and the stream gives object recognition, form and detail.",
          "start": 199.944,
          "end": 207.997
        },
        {
          "text": "And with that, both streams have been followed from V one to their destinations in the parietal and temporal cortex.",
          "start": 208.051,
          "end": 214.744
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
